# Functional test: an operator sets a tunnel's three per-SA options (df-bit clear,
# dscp-map 0, icmp-error-check false), and the REAL kernel's Child SA states carry
# each of them, read back through `show vpn ipsec dataplane sa`.
# Spec: plan/release/fixes/spec-rfc4301-architecture-gaps.md (RFC 4301 Sections 8.1,
#       5.1.2.1 and 6.2; user story 6). The commit-time halves are
#       test/parse/ipsec-df-bit-treatment.ci, test/parse/ipsec-dscp-map.ci,
#       test/parse/ipsec-icmp-error-check.ci and
#       test/ipsec/ipsec-dscp-map-linux-refused.ci.
#
# RFC 4301 Section 8.1: "This means that it MUST be possible to configure the
# implementation's treatment of the DF bit (set, clear, copy from inner header) for
# each SA.  This applies to SAs where both inner and outer headers are IPv4."
# RFC 4301 Section 5.1.2.1 note 5: "If the packet will immediately enter a domain for
# which the DSCP value in the outer header is not appropriate, that value MUST be
# mapped to an appropriate value for the domain".
# RFC 4301 Section 6.2: "Thus, an IPsec implementation MUST be configurable to check
# that this payload header information is consistent with the SA via which it
# arrives."
#
# VALIDATES: the three leaves of a tunnel-mode IPv4 responder reach the kernel. The
#            readback comes from the kernel's own flags (dataplane/xfrm_linux.go
#            saOptionsFromState), never from what the engine asked for:
#              - outbound state (src 127.0.0.2, dst 127.0.0.1): df-bit "clear", which
#                is XFRM_STATE_NOPMTUDISC (xfrmDFFlag), and dscp-map "0", which is
#                XFRM_SA_XFLAG_DONT_ENCAP_DSCP (xfrmDSCPFlag);
#              - inbound state (src 127.0.0.1, dst 127.0.0.2): icmp-error-check
#                false, the state without XFRM_STATE_ICMP (engine/child.go
#                `inbound.ICMPErrorCheck = !child.SkipICMPErrorCheck`).
#            Each asserted row also reads options-known true, so a backend that
#            never read the flags (null keys) cannot pass.
# PREVENTS: a clear or a 0 installed as copy (the outer header then follows the inner
#           packet the operator asked to override), and icmp-error-check false
#           installed as the default check, which drops the ICMP errors the operator
#           chose to accept.
#
# DEPENDS ON the df-bit, dscp-map, icmp-error-check and options-known keys of
# `show vpn ipsec dataplane sa` (cmd/show_dataplane.go saInfoToMap) and the kernel
# readback that fills them (SAInfo.Options, SAInfo.OptionsRead), which land with the
# same RFC 4301 work.
#
# WHY THE DAEMON STARTS WITH THE OPTIONS. Config verify judges a commit, and no verify
# runs on the configuration a daemon starts with, so these leaves reach the install
# path directly. Linux XFRM accepts all three values (SAOptionsSupported), so a
# commit would accept them too; the refusal side is ipsec-dscp-map-linux-refused.ci.
#
# WHY ONE DATAPLANE AND NOT TWO. Both ends of one Child SA are the same two kernel
# states, so a second XfrmStateAdd answers EEXIST. The RESPONDER runs the real xfrm
# backend and carries the options; the initiator runs the noop one
# (ipsec-show-dataplane-kernel.ci).
#
# WHY EACH ASSERTION BINDS ITS KEYS ON ONE ROW. An SA row's JSON keys are sorted
# (added-at, bytes, bytes-hard, df-bit, dscp-map, dst, encryption, ...,
# icmp-error-check, if-id, ..., options-known, ..., src, used-at), and no value of a
# row is an object, so `[^}]*` keeps one match inside one row. The ` ?` takes compact
# and indented JSON alike.
#
# WHY THESE PREFIXES. The policies are real and node-wide for the life of the test,
# so the selectors use RFC 2544 benchmarking ranges (198.18.248.0/23) no other ipsec
# test installs.
#
# RED PROOF, one break per option (each still installs the SA, so only the readback
# reds):
#   df-bit    in internal/component/ike/dataplane/xfrm_linux.go xfrmDFFlag, change
#             `case DFClear: return true, nil` to `return false, nil` (clear
#             installed as copy). The outbound row reads df-bit "copy": the first
#             assertion fails.
#   dscp-map  in xfrmDSCPFlag, change `if v == 0 { return true, nil }` to
#             `return false, nil` (0 installed as copy). The outbound row reads
#             dscp-map "copy": the first assertion fails.
#   icmp      in internal/component/ike/engine/child.go, change
#             `inbound.ICMPErrorCheck = !child.SkipICMPErrorCheck` to
#             `inbound.ICMPErrorCheck = true`. The inbound row reads
#             icmp-error-check true: the second assertion fails.
#
# Gated option=needs-linux:caps=net-admin,bpf: the responder programs real XFRM
# state, so this SKIPs on a host without CAP_NET_ADMIN and runs as root in the QEMU
# VM, where `fsuite ipsec` in internal/le/test/qemu/alltests.go executes the suite.

# bpf: the default `vpn ipsec application-override deny` loads a cgroup setsockopt guard.
option=needs-linux:caps=net-admin,bpf
# Serialize against every other real-XFRM ipsec test: the SAD is NODE-WIDE.
option=exclusive:group=ipsec-xfrm
option=env:var=ze_test_ike_port:value=$PORT2
option=timeout:value=120s
option=env:var=ze_log_ike:value=info

# ── Responder daemon (REAL xfrm, engine-steps executor): 127.0.0.2 ──
stdin=responder:terminator=EOF_RESPONDER
plugin {
	external engine-steps {
		run "le mock engine-steps ./engine-steps.json"
		encoder json
	}
}

vpn {
	ipsec {
		unmatched bypass
		esp-group esp-default {
			lifetime 3600
			proposal 1 {
				encryption aes256
				hash sha256
			}
		}
		ike-group ike-default {
			key-exchange ikev2
			lifetime 28800
			proposal 1 {
				dh-group 14
				encryption aes256
				hash sha256
			}
		}
		site-to-site {
			peer initiator {
				ike-group ike-default
				esp-group esp-default
				connection-type respond
				local-address 127.0.0.2
				remote-address 127.0.0.1
				mode tunnel
				df-bit clear
				dscp-map 0
				icmp-error-check false
				traffic-selector 1 {
					local {
						prefix 198.18.248.0/24
					}
					remote {
						prefix 198.18.249.0/24
					}
				}
				authentication {
					mode pre-shared-secret
					pre-shared-secret test-key-1234
				}
			}
		}
	}
}
EOF_RESPONDER

# ── Initiator daemon (background, noop dataplane): 127.0.0.1 ──
stdin=initiator:terminator=EOF_INITIATOR
vpn {
	ipsec {
		unmatched bypass
		esp-group esp-default {
			lifetime 3600
			proposal 1 {
				encryption aes256
				hash sha256
			}
		}
		ike-group ike-default {
			key-exchange ikev2
			lifetime 28800
			proposal 1 {
				dh-group 14
				encryption aes256
				hash sha256
			}
		}
		site-to-site {
			peer peer-1 {
				# Beside `unmatched bypass` or an ICMP SPD entry, the default
				# icmp-error-check true is refused by `ze config validate` and a commit,
				# and only logged at a start (verifyICMPOverlap); false keeps
				# this test's subject.
				icmp-error-check false
				ike-group ike-default
				esp-group esp-default
				connection-type initiate
				local-address 127.0.0.1
				remote-address 127.0.0.2
				mode tunnel
				traffic-selector 1 {
					local {
						prefix 198.18.249.0/24
					}
					remote {
						prefix 198.18.248.0/24
					}
				}
				authentication {
					mode pre-shared-secret
					pre-shared-secret test-key-1234
				}
			}
		}
	}
}
EOF_INITIATOR

cmd=background:seq=1:exec=env ze_test_ike_dataplane=noop ze -:stdin=initiator
# The responder carries the executor: the engine steps end with `request shutdown`,
# so this process exits when they are done, and teardown then stops the initiator.
cmd=foreground:seq=2:exec=ze -:stdin=responder:timeout=110s

# ── Engine steps, dispatched inside the responder ──

# Establishment gate. aes-cbc appears in the SA row only once IKE_AUTH has
# completed, and the responder installs both states before it answers IKE_AUTH.
command=show vpn ipsec sa | json
expect=output:contains=aes-cbc:timeout=60

# No `| json`: dispatch-command answers the JSON data field already, and the
# dispatcher does not strip a pipe, so on this command (it declares a spi leaf)
# the words after the pipe would be judged as arguments and refused.
command=show vpn ipsec dataplane sa
# The outbound state: df-bit clear and dscp-map 0, read from the kernel's flags.
expect=output:matches="df-bit": ?"clear",[^}]*"dscp-map": ?"0",[^}]*"dst": ?"127\.0\.0\.1",[^}]*"options-known": ?true,[^}]*"src": ?"127\.0\.0\.2":timeout=30
# The inbound state: icmp-error-check false, the state without XFRM_STATE_ICMP.
expect=output:matches="dst": ?"127\.0\.0\.2",[^}]*"icmp-error-check": ?false,[^}]*"options-known": ?true,[^}]*"src": ?"127\.0\.0\.1":timeout=10

expect=exit:code=0
expect=stderr:contains=engine-steps: all steps passed
reject=stderr:pattern=dataplane refused the ESP state
reject=stderr:pattern=panic recovered
reject=stderr:pattern=fatal error
